<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Twenty-seventh Dynasty of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as 27th Dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Twenty-seventh Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the name given to the first period of Persian rule over Egypt, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>'s conquest in 525 BCE. Achaemenid kings ruled Egypt as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> discuss it as part of the empire's foundational expansion, which brought Egypt into the largest empire the world had yet seen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/twenty-seventh-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Twenty-seventh Dynasty of Egypt" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mossmaps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_part_of_the_Achaemenid_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Twenty-seventh Dynasty, also called the First Egyptian Satrapy, was a satrapy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> between 525 and 404 BCE. Cambyses II founded it after defeating the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Twenty-sixth Dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelusium">Battle of Pelusium</a> in May 525 BCE, and was crowned pharaoh that summer at the latest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> to form the sixth satrapy of the empire, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryandes">Aryandes</a> as satrap. Under Cambyses the resources of the traditional temples diminished: a decree in demotic script limited the resources of all temples except those of Memphis, Heliopolis and Wenkhem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Cambyses died in 522 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> took the throne. Late in 522 or early in 521 BCE a local prince rebelled as Pharaoh Petubastis III; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a> blames oppressive taxation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryandes">Aryandes</a>. Darius reportedly codified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s laws, completed a canal from the Bitter Lakes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and supported Egyptian temples, earning a reputation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>. Aryandes was executed in 497 BCE, most likely for trying to issue his own coinage. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>, Egypt rebelled again; he installed his brother Achaemenes as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>, increased supply requirements and stopped funding Egyptian monuments. In 460 BCE the Libyan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inaros-ii">Inaros II</a>, substantially assisted by the Athenians, killed Achaemenes and took Memphis, but the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabyzus">Megabyzus</a> defeated him in 454 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Near the end of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii">Darius II</a> a rebellion led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyrtaeus">Amyrtaeus</a> took place. In 405 BCE, with the help of Cretan mercenaries, he expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> from Memphis and declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> the next year, ending the dynasty. Artaxerxes II planned an expedition to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> but abandoned it because of trouble with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a>. Three native dynasties, the 28th, 29th and 30th, then ruled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> reconquered Egypt in 343 BCE, a brief second period known as the Thirty-first Dynasty. A 2019 analysis by H. P. Colburn finds the Achaemenid legacy in Egypt significant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Twenty-seventh Dynasty of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Cambyses II defeated Pharaoh Psamtik III at the Battle of Pelusium in 525 BCE and incorporated Egypt, where the Persian kings count as its <b>27th Dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
